{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Generally, **no**. An Irish ICAV is a single legal entity; its sub-funds are not separate legal persons and cannot usually contract or sue in their own names. The ICAV acts for them.\n\nBut Irish law provides **statutory segregation of liability** between sub-funds: assets attributable to one sub-fund should be used only to meet that sub-fund’s liabilities, not another’s. This is often called “protected cell” or ring-fenced liability, but it does **not** give each sub-fund separate legal personality. The ICAV remains the contracting party, and the segregation’s practical effect can depend on proper allocation and recognition of the structure, especially in cross-border situations.\n\nThis is general information, not legal advice; check the ICAV’s constitutional documents and relevant agreements."}}
